PART 3: FIXING THE COUNTER COMPONENT
================================================================================

BEFORE: COUNTER COMPONENT WITH THE WRONG PATTERN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

import { useState } from 'react'


export default function Counter() {

  // ❌ WRONG - Regular local variable

  let count = 0


  const handleIncrement = () => {

    count = count + 1

    console.log('Count:', count)
  }


  const handleDecrement = () => {

    count = count - 1

    console.log('Count:', count)
  }


  const handleReset = () => {

    count = 0

    console.log('Count:', count)
  }


  return (
    <div className="counter">

      <h1>
        Counter: {count}
      </h1>

      <p>
        Console can show local variable changes,
        but those changes are not React state updates.
      </p>

      <button onClick={handleIncrement}>
        Increment
      </button>

      <button onClick={handleDecrement}>
        Decrement
      </button>

      <button onClick={handleReset}>
        Reset
      </button>

    </div>
  )
}


PROBLEMS WITH THIS APPROACH
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• No useState Hook
  - count is a regular local variable

• No State Setter
  - React isn't being asked to update component state

• Local Variable Doesn't Persist as React State
  - A render initializes the function's local variables again

• UI Isn't Driven by Managed State
  - Changing count alone doesn't request a React render

• Console Can Be Misleading
  - JavaScript variable changed, but React state did not

• Harder to Build Reliable Interactivity
  - State should represent information remembered across renders


================================================================================
AFTER: COUNTER COMPONENT WITH useState
================================================================================

import { useState } from 'react'


export default function Counter() {

  // ✓ CORRECT - React state

  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)


  // Increment count

  const handleIncrement = () => {
    setCount(count + 1)
  }


  // Decrement count

  const handleDecrement = () => {
    setCount(count - 1)
  }


  // Reset count

  const handleReset = () => {
    setCount(0)
  }


  return (
    <div className="counter">

      <h1>
        Counter: {count}
      </h1>

      <p>
        UI updates when React processes the state changes.
      </p>

      <button onClick={handleIncrement}>
        Increment
      </button>

      <button onClick={handleDecrement}>
        Decrement
      </button>

      <button onClick={handleReset}>
        Reset
      </button>

    </div>
  )
}


LINE-BY-LINE EXPLANATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+--------------------------------------+---------------------------------------+
| CODE / CONCEPT                       | WHAT IT DOES                          |
+--------------------------------------+---------------------------------------+
| import { useState }                  | Imports the useState Hook             |
| const [count, setCount]              | Creates count state                   |
| useState(0)                          | Initializes count to 0                |
| handleIncrement                      | Defines increment handler             |
| setCount(count + 1)                  | Requests count + 1 update             |
| handleDecrement                      | Defines decrement handler             |
| setCount(count - 1)                  | Requests count - 1 update             |
| handleReset                          | Defines reset handler                 |
| setCount(0)                          | Requests reset to 0                   |
| Counter: {count}                     | Displays current state                |
| onClick={handleIncrement}            | Connects button to handler            |
+--------------------------------------+---------------------------------------+


WHAT CHANGED?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• Regular variable → useState

• Direct assignment → State setter

• Local value → Persistent component state

• No state update → React-managed state update

• Static/stale UI → State-driven UI

• Unreliable behavior → Predictable state flow


HOW IT WORKS NOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

User Clicks Increment
        ↓
handleIncrement()
        ↓
setCount(count + 1)
        ↓
React Processes State Update
        ↓
Component Renders with New count
        ↓
Updated UI


================================================================================
FUNCTIONAL UPDATE - RECOMMENDED WHEN DEPENDING ON PREVIOUS STATE
================================================================================

Instead of:

setCount(count + 1)


You can use:

setCount(previousCount =>
  previousCount + 1
)


Similarly:

const handleDecrement = () => {

  setCount(previousCount =>
    previousCount - 1
  )
}


WHY USE THIS?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

When the next state depends on the previous state, the functional setter:

• Receives the previous state value

• Makes the dependency explicit

• Is especially useful when multiple updates may be queued


Example:

setCount(count => count + 1)
setCount(count => count + 1)
setCount(count => count + 1)


Each updater receives the result of the preceding queued update.


