AFTER: USERPROFILE WITH useState
================================================================================

import { useState } from 'react'


export default function UserProfile() {

  const [user, setUser] = useState({
    name: 'John Doe',
    email: 'john@example.com',
    age: 30
  })


  // Update name immutably

  const handleNameChange = (newName) => {

    setUser({
      ...user,
      name: newName
    })
  }


  // Update email immutably

  const handleEmailChange = (newEmail) => {

    setUser({
      ...user,
      email: newEmail
    })
  }


  // Update age immutably

  const handleAgeChange = (newAge) => {

    setUser({
      ...user,
      age: parseInt(newAge)
    })
  }


  return (
    <div className="profile">

      <h2>{user.name}</h2>

      <p>
        Email: {user.email}
      </p>

      <p>
        Age: {user.age}
      </p>


      <input
        value={user.name}
        onChange={(e) =>
          handleNameChange(e.target.value)
        }
        placeholder="Enter name"
      />


      <input
        type="email"
        value={user.email}
        onChange={(e) =>
          handleEmailChange(e.target.value)
        }
        placeholder="Enter email"
      />


      <input
        type="number"
        value={user.age}
        onChange={(e) =>
          handleAgeChange(e.target.value)
        }
        placeholder="Enter age"
      />


      <p>
        UI updates when user state changes.
      </p>

    </div>
  )
}


OBJECT UPDATE PATTERN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Existing State:

{
  name: 'John Doe',
  email: 'john@example.com',
  age: 30
}


Update:

setUser({
  ...user,
  name: 'Jane Doe'
})


Result:

{
  name: 'Jane Doe',
  email: 'john@example.com',
  age: 30
}


The spread syntax:

...user

copies the existing properties, and:

name: 'Jane Doe'

overrides only the name.


LINE-BY-LINE EXPLANATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+
| CODE / CONCEPT                   | WHAT IT DOES                              |
+----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+
| useState({...})                  | Creates user object state                 |
| user                             | Current user object                       |
| setUser                          | User state setter                         |
| ...user                          | Copies current object properties         |
| name: newName                    | Replaces name in new object              |
| email: newEmail                  | Replaces email in new object             |
| age: parseInt(newAge)            | Converts input string to number          |
| value={user.name}                | Makes name input controlled              |
| onChange                         | Responds to input changes                |
+----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+


WHAT CHANGED?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• Regular Object → React State

• Direct Mutation → Immutable Replacement

• user.name = value → setUser({...user, name: value})

• Uncontrolled Display Flow → Controlled Input

• Existing Reference → New User Object


HOW IT WORKS NOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

User Types
    ↓
onChange
    ↓
handleNameChange()
    ↓
Create Updated user Object
    ↓
setUser()
    ↓
React Processes State Update
    ↓
Component Re-renders
    ↓
Input + Display Reflect Updated State


NOTE ABOUT AGE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Input values arrive as strings.

This example uses:

parseInt(newAge)

to convert the age to a number.

For production code, also consider the empty-input case because:

parseInt('')

returns:

NaN


