PART 7: COMMON useState PATTERNS
================================================================================

PATTERN 1: SIMPLE COUNTER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const [count, setCount] = useState(0)


const increment = () =>
  setCount(count + 1)


const decrement = () =>
  setCount(count - 1)


const reset = () =>
  setCount(0)


Functional-update version:

const increment = () =>
  setCount(count => count + 1)


const decrement = () =>
  setCount(count => count - 1)


================================================================================
PATTERN 2: TOGGLE BOOLEAN
================================================================================

const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false)


const toggle = () =>
  setIsOpen(previous =>
    !previous
  )


================================================================================
PATTERN 3: FORM INPUT
================================================================================

const [input, setInput] = useState('')


const handleChange = (e) =>
  setInput(e.target.value)


const handleReset = () =>
  setInput('')


================================================================================
PATTERN 4: LIST MANAGEMENT
================================================================================

const [items, setItems] = useState([])


const addItem = (item) =>
  setItems([
    ...items,
    item
  ])


const removeItem = (id) =>
  setItems(
    items.filter(item =>
      item.id !== id
    )
  )


================================================================================
PATTERN 5: OBJECT UPDATE
================================================================================

const [user, setUser] = useState({
  name: '',
  email: ''
})


const updateUser = (field, value) =>

  setUser({
    ...user,
    value
  })


Example:

updateUser('name', 'John')


Result:

{
  name: 'John',
  email: ''
}


================================================================================
PATTERN 6: CONDITIONAL / STATUS STATE
================================================================================

const [status, setStatus] = useState('idle')


// Possible values:
// 'idle'
// 'loading'
// 'success'
// 'error'


const startLoading = () =>
  setStatus('loading')


const setSuccess = () =>
  setStatus('success')


const setError = () =>
  setStatus('error')


================================================================================
PATTERN 7: MULTIPLE STATE VARIABLES
================================================================================

const [firstName, setFirstName] = useState('')

const [lastName, setLastName] = useState('')

const [email, setEmail] = useState('')


Use separate state variables when the values are reasonably independent.


================================================================================
PATTERN 8: FUNCTIONAL STATE UPDATE
================================================================================

const [count, setCount] = useState(0)


const increment = () => {

  setCount(previousCount =>
    previousCount + 1
  )
}


Use this when the next state depends on the previous state.


================================================================================
COMMON PATTERNS TABLE
================================================================================

+--------------------+------------------------+-------------------------------+
| PATTERN            | USE CASE               | CORE IDEA                     |
+--------------------+------------------------+-------------------------------+
| Counter            | Increment/decrement    | setCount(...)                 |
| Toggle             | Show/hide, on/off      | setIsOpen(prev => !prev)      |
| Form Input         | Controlled input       | setInput(e.target.value)      |
| List Management    | Add/remove items       | Spread/filter                 |
| Object Update      | Change object field    | {...user, field: value}       |
| Status State       | Async/UI status        | setStatus('loading')          |
| Multiple Variables | Independent values     | Multiple useState Hooks       |
| Functional Update  | Depends on previous    | setter(prev => next)          |
+--------------------+------------------------+-------------------------------+


IMPORTANT: DERIVED VALUES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

If a value can be calculated directly from existing state/props,
you often do NOT need another state variable.

Example:

const [price, setPrice] = useState(10)
const [quantity, setQuantity] = useState(2)


Instead of:

const [total, setTotal] = useState(20)


Calculate:

const total = price * quantity


This avoids duplicated state.


================================================================================
PART 8: FIXING COMMON useState MISTAKES
================================================================================

MISTAKE 1: FORGETTING TO CALL THE STATE SETTER
================================================================================

WRONG:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

let count = 0

count = count + 1


CORRECT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const [count, setCount] = useState(0)

setCount(count + 1)


================================================================================
MISTAKE 2: MUTATING OBJECT STATE
================================================================================

WRONG:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const [user, setUser] = useState({
  name: 'John'
})


user.name = 'Jane'


CORRECT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setUser({
  ...user,
  name: 'Jane'
})


================================================================================
MISTAKE 3: MUTATING ARRAY STATE
================================================================================

WRONG:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const [items, setItems] = useState([])

items.push(item)


CORRECT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setItems([
  ...items,
  item
])


================================================================================
MISTAKE 4: REUSING A MUTATED REFERENCE
================================================================================

WRONG:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const [data, setData] = useState({
  count: 0
})


data.count++

setData(data)


Problem:

The existing state object was mutated and then reused.


CORRECT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setData({
  ...data,
  count: data.count + 1
})


================================================================================
MISTAKE 5: EXPECTING THE CURRENT HANDLER'S STATE VARIABLE TO CHANGE
IMMEDIATELY AFTER CALLING THE SETTER
================================================================================

MISLEADING EXPECTATION:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const [count, setCount] = useState(0)


const increment = () => {

  setCount(count + 1)

  console.log(count)

  // Still logs the count value from
  // the current render
}


WHY?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Calling setCount() requests an update.

It does NOT rewrite the count variable inside the already-running
event handler.


IF YOU NEED THE CALCULATED NEXT VALUE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const increment = () => {

  const newCount = count + 1

  setCount(newCount)

  console.log(newCount)
}


IF THE UPDATE DEPENDS ON PREVIOUS STATE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setCount(previous =>
  previous + 1
)


COMMON MISTAKES TABLE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+----------------------+--------------------------+-----------------------------+
| MISTAKE              | WRONG                    | CORRECT                     |
+----------------------+--------------------------+-----------------------------+
| Regular variable     | count = count + 1        | setCount(count + 1)         |
| Mutating object      | user.name = newName      | {...user, name: newName}    |
| Mutating array       | items.push(item)         | [...items, item]            |
| Reuse mutation       | setData(data) after      | Create new object           |
|                      | modifying data           |                             |
| Nested overwrite     | Lose sibling fields      | Spread each changed level   |
| Sort mutation        | setArr(arr.sort())       | setArr([...arr].sort())     |
| Missing list key     | <Item item={i} />        | key={i.id}                  |
+----------------------+--------------------------+-----------------------------+


NOTE ABOUT key:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The key prop is important for list rendering, but it is NOT a useState
update mechanism.

State update:
setItems(...)

List identity:
key={item.id}

These solve different problems.


================================================================================
PART 9: useState BEST PRACTICES
================================================================================

BEST PRACTICES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+
| PRACTICE             | WHY IT MATTERS                                |
+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+
| Descriptive names    | Makes state purpose obvious                   |
| Keep state minimal   | Reduces synchronization problems              |
| Immutable updates    | Avoids modifying previous state               |
| Initialize properly  | Provides predictable first render             |
| Functional updates   | Best when next value depends on previous      |
| Group related state  | Useful when values change together            |
| Avoid deep nesting   | Makes immutable updates simpler               |
| filter()/map()       | Useful immutable array transformations        |
| Document structure   | Helps with complex state                      |
| Test behavior        | Confirms UI responds to state changes         |
+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+


BEST PRACTICE 1: USE DESCRIPTIVE NAMES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Less clear:

const [open, setOpen] = useState(false)


Clearer:

const [isMenuOpen, setIsMenuOpen] = useState(false)


================================================================================
BEST PRACTICE 2: KEEP STATE MINIMAL
================================================================================

Store only information that must be remembered across renders.

Example:

const [price, setPrice] = useState(100)
const [quantity, setQuantity] = useState(2)


Calculate:

const total = price * quantity


instead of storing another synchronized total state when unnecessary.


================================================================================
BEST PRACTICE 3: USE IMMUTABLE UPDATES
================================================================================

Object:

setUser({
  ...user,
  name: newName
})


Array:

setItems([
  ...items,
  newItem
])


================================================================================
BEST PRACTICE 4: USE FUNCTIONAL UPDATES WHEN DEPENDING ON PREVIOUS STATE
================================================================================

Prefer:

setCount(previous =>
  previous + 1
)


For arrays:

setItems(previousItems => [
  ...previousItems,
  newItem
])


For objects:

setUser(previousUser => ({
  ...previousUser,
  name: newName
}))


================================================================================
BEST PRACTICE 5: KEEP STATE STRUCTURE MANAGEABLE
================================================================================

Complex deeply nested state:

state.level1.level2.level3.property


requires copying every changed level.

Where practical, choose state structures that are easier to update.


